{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 27, :comments ["<b>And see with your eyes.</b> It was necessary to say the word <b>with your eyes</b> — and all the more so after He said immediately nearby ״lift up your eyes.״ He intended to tell him that what he asked to see by means of going to the land, as he said ״Let me cross over and see,״ HaShem would perform for him a wonder, and he would see with his very eyes alone, without going, everything that he asked to see once he crossed. And this is something that the eye of any other person could not see — and this is what He scrutinized by saying ״your eyes״ with the possessive suffix." "<b>Further,</b> it intends that there are scholars who bring distant things near by means of an intermediary device that they place between their eyes facing distant places, thereby bringing them near and seeing them as though they were before him. To exclude this, HaShem said to him ״And see with your eyes״ — literally, meaning without the intermediacy of any other thing."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 3, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:14:51.574753285Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}